2/5 unilateral weakness)", "Exercise-induced nerve compression (plausible but lacks objective evidence)"], "alarm_features": ["Significant weight loss", "Chronic heavy tobacco use", "Anabolic steroid use"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Brachial Plexopathy / Neuroinflammatory Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.4793777817018698, "posterior": 0.42197932637618096, "danger": 0.65, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Lack of positional symptom variability strongly argues against neurogenic TOS, while absence of vascular signs lessens suspicion for subclavian steal syndrome, though the latter has limited diagnostic certainty.", "In a middle-aged patient without claudication or cardiac history, vascular insufficiency is unlikely to be the primary mechanism despite his bodybuilding background."], "evidence_against": ["Normal anatomical findings in the cervical spine region do not definitively rule out neuroinflammatory pathology; they merely reduce suspicion for structural compression from neoplastic causes."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3", "B1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of trauma or repetitive arm strain", "Pattern of sensory loss (distal vs proximal)", "Progression timeline (sudden vs gradual)"], "requestable_discriminators": ["MRI with contrast focusing on brachial plexus", "Nerve conduction studies with needle EMG", "CT chest / apical lung imaging"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Thoracic Outlet Syndrome with Apical Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.036621142740184534, "posterior": 0.03223629824846504, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Chronic heavy tobacco use and significant weight loss are moderate risk factors for malignant apical neoplasms (e.g., Pancoast tumors), which align with the 'Thoracic Outlet Syndrome with Apical Neoplasm' branch but do not directly indicate malignancy."], "evidence_against": ["Normal anatomical findings in the cervical spine region do not definitively rule out neuroinflammatory pathology; they merely reduce suspicion for structural compression from neoplastic causes.", "Lack of positional symptom variability strongly argues against neurogenic TOS, while absence of vascular signs lessens suspicion for subclavian steal syndrome, though the latter has limited diagnostic certainty."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of apical cough/hemoptysis", "Arm swelling/vascular symptoms with overhead activity", "Radiation to shoulder/neck"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Chest X-ray (including apical region)", "Subclavian artery Doppler ultrasound", "Apical lung biopsy if imaging suspicious"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.35, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Vascular Insufficiency Syndrome", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.03391435212989198, "danger": 0.55, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["In a middle-aged patient without claudication or cardiac history, vascular insufficiency is unlikely to be the primary mechanism despite his bodybuilding background."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Cervical Spine Disease with Radiation/Pathology", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.03391435212989198, "danger": 0.45, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Neuroinflammatory / Neurodegenerative Condition", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.02374004649092439, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Primary Neoplasm of the Brachial Plexus", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.1922943765764875, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Focal weakness and dysesthesia favor a central/inflammatory myelopathy over focal peripheral neoplasm, though direct evidence remains insufficient."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Neuroinflammatory Myelopathy with Brachial Radiation", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.14422078243236564, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal CNS function (no bowel/bladder deficits) provides moderate reassurance against spinal cord pathology in B1.2 despite focal neuroinflammatory-like symptoms."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Steroid-Induced Neuroinflammatory Syndrome", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.05341510460457986, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.4": {"id": "B1.4", "label": "Paraneoplastic Syndrome (Brachial Plexus Involvement)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.036408439116597704, "posterior": 0.03204906276274792, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Burning pain may align with neuroinflammatory processes; steroid modulation alone does not decisively distinguish between primary neoplasm and paraneoplastic mechanisms."], "evidence_against": ["Burning pain is consistent with neuroinflammatory processes in brachial plexus disorders, though the absence of documented systemic inflammation prevents stronger support.", "Lack of expected paraneoplastic features (lymphadenopathy, systemic signs) moderately weakens B1.4 but does not confirm B1.1 due to incomplete exclusion requirements."], "unresolved_questions": [], "chil